Just "fg" by itself will work fine in all common shells (including sh/
bash/ksh/csh/tcsh and any POSIX-compliant shell with job control). "fg
nethack" fails in some common shells (e.g. csh/tcsh) so isn't a good
general recommendation

"fg nethack" in sh/bash/ksh _might_ do the trick if you have started
and suspended more programs in the interim, but might not--it depends
on what those programs were.  In general for complex situations you
want "jobs" and "fg %#", where # is a job number.



